    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1130">

                <pb facs="1168" n="1156" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg1156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/7–845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-08T16:00:00+02:00">July 8, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:32 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4100. The following communiqué issued by FonOff: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In accordance with a declaration made at Beirut<anchor n="54"
                                xml:id="d1130-f1"/> by Gen. Beynet, French Delegate General and
                            Plenipotentiary in the Levant, it has been decided in view of the end of
                            hostilities in Europe to agree to the desire of Syria and Lebanon to
                            establish a national army, and consequently to transfer to the Syrian
                            and Lebanese Govts the locally recruited military units which are under
                            French control.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d1130-f2"/> The details of the
                            transfer will be settled within 45 days.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Figaro</hi> comments on above that “French diplomatic
                    circles” consider that it should be interpreted as indicating a French desire to
                    reestablish cordial relations with Syria and Lebanon and to establish their
                    independence fully and definitely, thus contradicting certain rumors current
                    abroad.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept as 4100 rptd Beirut as 44 and London as 502.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1130-f1" n="54" xml:id="d1130-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably, the communiqué issued on July 8 by the Delegation Generate; for text,
                    see telegram 211, July 9, 2 a.m., from Beirut, <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_968">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Conference of Berlin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Potsdam</hi>), 1945, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p.
                        968</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1130-f2" n="55" xml:id="d1130-f2-content">
                <p>For accounts by Mr. Wadsworth of his discussions with Count Ostrorog and Major
                    General Pilleau on the transfer of the <hi rendition="#italic">Troupes
                        Spéciales</hi> and other matters, see his telegrams 208, July 7, 3 p.m., and
                    213, July 9, 7 p.m., <ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_967">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>, pp. 967</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_969">969</ref>; for report by the Ambassador in
                    the United Kingdom on remarks made by a “FonOff official” on the situation in
                    the Levant, see his telegram 6908, July 9, 8 p.m., <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_970">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 970</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
